  <p>
    RFC-0005 is reserved and unwritten. RFC-0013 (<a
      href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi/pull/76">#76</a
    >) reached <code>main</code> in the 8 October release, and it stays a draft until the
    owner accepts it. Beside the RFCs,{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi/blob/main/design/LANGUAGE-SURVEY.md"
      ><code>design/LANGUAGE-SURVEY.md</code></a
    > surveys the top 10 languages feature by feature
    as design input for RFC-0013 and the tier RFCs after it. Nothing in it is
    implemented or measured. The Mzizi Roots components have their own
    RFC in the registry:{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi-registry/blob/main/docs/roots/RFC-roots.md"
      ><code>mzizi-registry/docs/roots/RFC-roots.md</code></a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trying-it">Trying it</h2>

  <p>
    The toolchain's compiler, which implements the language, is the{" "}
    <code>mz</code> binary in{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi">mzizi-dev/mzizi</a>. Building it
    is one command against a checkout — no toolchain beyond stable Rust:
  </p>

  <code class="cmd lead">cargo build --release --manifest-path compiler/Cargo.toml</code>

  <p>
    The surface an agent should use is <code>mz check --agent</code>: whole-program
    NDJSON in a deterministic order, at most one diagnostic per real error, every
    message written for a reader with no prior context, and machine-applicable
    fixes tagged <code>exact</code> or <code>guess</code>. Below is real, captured
    output — not a mock-up — from running it against{" "}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi/blob/main/examples/connectivity_bar.mz"
      ><code>examples/connectivity_bar.mz</code></a
    >, one of the repository's two component examples, at{" "}
    <a href={`https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi/commit/${LANG_COMMIT}`}><code>{LANG_COMMIT}</code></a
    >.
  </p>

  <p>Clean input, run through the whole pipeline:</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <p class="terminal-cmd">$ mz check --agent examples/connectivity_bar.mz</p>
    <pre><code>{`{"summary":true,"errors":0,"warnings":0,"exact_fixable":0,"ms":0}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    The same file with one real mistake — a dropped colon on a typed{" "}
    <code>prop</code> — run through the identical command:
  </p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <p class="terminal-cmd">$ mz check --agent examples/connectivity_bar.mz</p>
    <pre><code>{`{"code":"MZ0305","severity":"error","file":"examples/connectivity_bar.mz","span":[15,16,15,20],"say":"\`prop visible\` needs a type: write \`prop visible: <type>\`","fix":{"span":[15,15,15,15],"replace":": bool","confidence":"guess"}}
{"summary":true,"errors":1,"warnings":0,"exact_fixable":0,"ms":0}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    One diagnostic, one line, the exact character span, a plain-language
    explanation, and a machine-applicable fix an agent can apply without asking a
    human to interpret the error first — that is the dense-error design goal
    above, not as a claim but as something you can reproduce from the commit
    linked above. Other useful commands against the same file:
  </p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <p class="terminal-cmd">$ mz hash examples/connectivity_bar.mz</p>
    <pre><code>{`40c3b85d31c1  connectivity_bar  23 nodes`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>mz fix</code> applies every <code>exact</code> fix in one pass and
    re-checks; <code>mz outline</code> and <code>mz ir</code> exist too. Run{" "}
    <code>mz</code> with no arguments for its usage line. What is not there yet:{" "}
    <code>mz refs</code>, <code>mz path</code>, <code>mz patch</code> and{" "}
    <code>mz diff</code> are designed (see the RFCs) but not built, and so are{" "}
    <code>mz test</code> and <code>mz fmt</code>. The binary above dispatches{" "}
    <code>check</code>, <code>fix</code>, <code>contract</code>,{" "}
    <code>outline</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>run</code> and{" "}
    <code>harness</code>, plus <code>hash</code> and <code>ir</code> for
    reading the IR.
  </p>

  <h3 id="programs">A program, with <code>mz run</code></h3>

  <p>
    A <code>program</code> file holds functions, with <code>fn main</code> as
    its entry point. <code>mz run</code> checks it, lowers it to a Cargo package
    with no dependencies, builds it with <code>cargo build --offline</code> and
    runs it, exiting with the program's status. Integer overflow and division
    by zero stop the program with <code>MZ0991</code> and exit 101; a{" "}
    <code>float</code> follows IEEE 754 and never traps. Below is{" "}
    <code>examples/numbers.mz</code>, unedited, and what <code>mz run</code>{" "}
    printed for it at <code>{LANG_COMMIT}</code>. CI runs it and the other
    example programs (<code>hello</code>, <code>fib</code>,{" "}
    <code>control</code> for loops and <code>match</code>, and{" "}
    <code>errors</code> for <code>result</code> and <code>try</code>) and
    compares each with its committed <code>.expected</code> file.
  </p>

  <ProgramRun program={numbersProgram} />

  <p>
    Records (RFC-0013 §11, C8) are the next example: <code>examples/records.mz</code>,
    unedited, with the output <code>mz run</code> printed for it at{" "}
    <code>{LANG_COMMIT}</code>. Its fields are read and assigned, its methods read{" "}
    <code>self</code>, and its <code>always</code> clause holds for every value.
    CI compares it with <code>examples/records.expected</code>.
  </p>

  <ProgramRun program={recordsProgram} />

  <h3 id="harness">The language harness, with <code>mz harness</code></h3>

  <p>
    The language harness is the spine of the language (RFC-0012): every
    feature is defined once, as an entry in the compiler, and that entry is
    what an agent reads. Its first slice is built.{" "}
    <code>compiler/src/harness.rs</code> registers 191 entries on{" "}
    <code>main</code> at <code>{LANG_COMMIT}</code>: the <code>program</code> kind and everything a
    program has (types, operators, methods, statement forms,{" "}
    <code>result</code> and <code>try</code>), the nine <code>mz</code>{" "}
    commands, and 80 diagnostic codes, each with its <code>say</code> text, fix
    kinds and a trigger. <code>component</code> and <code>service</code> are
    registered at kind level only, and their 49 codes are on a pending list
    that a test lets only shrink.
  </p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <p class="terminal-cmd">$ mz harness version</p>
    <pre><code>{`{"protocol":1,"language":"phase-0, RFC-0013 wave 0, wave 1 numbers, control flow and errors","definition_sha256":"ca44fa70aa98f36fed6ca0bc7824c594aca6620cc4b5195a6ecc47c73ad2b07b"}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>mz harness definition</code> prints every entry as JSON, and{" "}
    <code>mz harness entry &lt;name&gt;</code> prints one
    (<code>mz harness entry try</code>, <code>mz harness entry MZ0950</code>).
    What is enforced: <code>mz</code> dispatches on the harness's command table;
    the program parser and checker read the type names, operators and method
    signatures from the same tables; and tests fail when the compiler can emit
    a code with no entry, when a trigger stops raising its code with a declared
    fix kind, or when an example stops checking or printing its stated output.
    A pull request that adds a feature must add its entry. What is written by
    hand and not compared with the checker: each code's <code>say</code> text
    and each feature's teaching text. Not built: the plugin host, and the
    skills and benchmark guides generated from the definition. Whether an
    agent taught by the harness does better is a hypothesis the benchmark is
    designed to test (RFC-0012 §9); nothing has measured it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>mz build</code> takes a service only. Against the repository's example
    service,{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi/blob/main/examples/registry.mz"
      ><code>examples/registry.mz</code></a
    >, at <code>{LANG_COMMIT}</code>:
  </p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <p class="terminal-cmd">$ mz contract examples/registry.mz</p>
    <pre><code>{`mz: 0 errors (0 exact-fixable), 22 contract clauses, 0 failed, tested over 61 generated requests, 1ms`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="terminal">
    <p class="terminal-cmd">$ mz build examples/registry.mz --out out/registry</p>
    <pre><code>{buildOutput}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    The output is an ordinary Cargo package on axum and tokio, which CI builds,
    tests and serves. It runs locally; there is no Workers, Containers or
    WebAssembly target yet, and no component lowers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start with <a href={CHARTER}>CHARTER.md</a> (v0.4, "Mzizi: a
    general-purpose programming language"), which carries the full thesis, the
    phasing, the resolved benchmark decisions and the questions still open, and
    then <a href={TRACKER}>LANGUAGE-TRACKER.md</a> for what still has to be
    built.
  </p>
